<Project>

  <!--
    Where cbexigen and the schema disagree, follow the schema.

    Two constructs in ISO 15118 are encoded differently by cbexigen (and therefore by cbV2G, EVerest
    and tux-evse) than a schema-informed EXI processor built from the specification encodes them. Both
    were found on 2026-08-07, when EXIficient became the first codec other than ours to read our -20
    corpus and six frames did not read at all:

      * the document grammar's numbering of global elements, when two of them share a named type
        (Grammar/DocumentElementOrder.cs) — in ISO 15118 that is ACDP and nothing else;
      * an optional repeating element followed by an optional one
        (Grammar/ParticleGrammar.cs) — in ISO 15118 that is WPT FinePositioning and nothing else.

    This codec used to reproduce cbexigen in both places, because matching the reference encoder
    byte-for-byte is how the vector corpus earns its keep. Decided on 2026-08-08 to stop doing so:

      * The specification is not ambiguous about either. For the numbering, asking EXIficient to encode
        a three-element schema in the ACDP shape — two elements sharing a type, a third sorting between
        them — gives plain lexicographic indices 0/1/2; cbexigen groups the pair and yields 0/2/1. For
        the particle grammar, cbexigen contradicts its *own input schema*: it caps a maxOccurs="16"
        list at two and hides the following particle from the empty state, so two perfectly valid
        documents cannot be encoded at all.
      * The failure mode is bad. Our ACDP_ConnectRes decodes cleanly, as ACDP_DisconnectReq — the
        wrong message, with nothing to report it.
      * Nothing is on the wire. ACDP and WPT are exactly the message sets for which no independent
        stack implements a session state machine, cbexigen-derived ones included. The byte
        compatibility being given up here is compatibility with an encoder nobody runs for these
        messages.
      * The evidence improves. The affected vectors were cbV2G's output checked against cbV2G; they
        are now verified through EXIficient, which shares no line with cbexigen.

    Both remain switches, not one-way doors: set either property back to CbV2GCompatible to get the
    cbexigen bytes. The generator's own defaults are still CbV2GCompatible — this file is the ISO
    codecs' decision, not the generator's.

    Byte-neutral outside ACDP and WPT by construction (the orders coincide unless a type is shared;
    the particle rule applies to one construct), and the vector corpus is what proves it: every other
    expected byte in the solution is unchanged.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ExiDocumentElementOrder>ExiSorted</ExiDocumentElementOrder>
    <ExiParticleGrammar>SchemaConformant</ExiParticleGrammar>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
